   "source": [
    "# Module 06 Lab - Regression and Classification Models\n",
    "\n",
    "**Objective:** To understand the difference between regression and classification and to build your first linear models for both tasks.\n",
    "\n",
    "**In this lab, you will write the code to train and evaluate the models.**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "73c6ce45",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "73c6ce45"
   },
   "source": [
    "## Part 1: Regression vs. Classification\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the most fundamental distinction between types of supervised learning problems.\n",
    "\n",
    "*   **Regression:** The goal is to predict a **continuous numerical value**.\n",
    "    *   *Examples:* Predicting the price of a house, the temperature tomorrow, or the stock price.\n",
    "\n",
    "*   **Classification:** The goal is to predict a **discrete category or class label**.\n",
    "    *   *Examples:* Predicting if an email is spam or not spam, if a flower is a setosa, versicolor, or virginica, or if a customer will churn or not.\n",
    "\n",
    "**In this lab, we will tackle one of each.**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "eb765bb0",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "eb765bb0"
   },
   "source": [
    "## Part 2: Linear Regression\n",
    "\n",
    "**Concept:** Linear Regression is used to predict a continuous value. It works by finding the best-fitting straight line through the data points. The model learns a \"slope\" (coefficient) for each feature and an \"intercept\".\n",
    "\n",
    "*   **Problem:** We will predict the `Fare` of a Titanic passenger based on their `Age` and `Pclass`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "fec42c84",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "fec42c84"
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
    "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error\n",
    "\n",
    "# Load and prepare the data\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/datasciencedojo/datasets/master/titanic.csv')\n",
    "\n",
    "# For simplicity, we'll drop rows with missing age\n",
    "df.dropna(subset=['Age'], inplace=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Define features and target\n",
    "features = ['Age', 'Pclass']\n",
    "target_reg = 'Fare'\n",
    "\n",
    "X_reg = df[features]\n",
    "y_reg = df[target_reg]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Split the data\n",
    "X_train_reg, X_test_reg, y_train_reg, y_test_reg = train_test_split(X_reg, y_reg, test_size=0.2, random_state=42)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7c059756",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "7c059756"
   },
   "source": [
    "### Task 1: Train and Evaluate a Linear Regression Model\n",
    "\n",
    "**Your Task:**\n",
    "1.  Create an instance of the `LinearRegression` model.\n",
    "2.  Train the model using the training data (`X_train_reg`, `y_train_reg`).\n",
    "3.  Make predictions on the test data.\n",
    "4.  Evaluate the model using `mean_squared_error`. This metric tells us the average of the squared differences between the predicted and actual values."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "89e585f3",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "89e585f3",
    "outputId": "5c7b4808-447e-4cf7-b887-6138979c8fc0"
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Mean Squared Error for Fare Prediction: 3364.92\n",
      "The square root of this is 58.01, meaning our model is off by about $58.01 on average.\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "\n",
    "# 1. Create the model instance\n",
    "lr_model = LinearRegression()\n",
    "\n",
    "# 2. Train the model\n",
    "lr_model.fit(X_train_reg, y_train_reg)\n",
    "\n",
    "# 3. Make predictions\n",
    "y_pred_reg = lr_model.predict(X_test_reg)\n",
    "\n",
    "# 4. Evaluate the model\n",
    "mse = mean_squared_error(y_test_reg, y_pred_reg)\n",
    "print(f\"Mean Squared Error for Fare Prediction: {mse:.2f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"The square root of this is {np.sqrt(mse):.2f}, meaning our model is off by about ${np.sqrt(mse):.2f} on average.\")"
   ]
  },
