    "## Part 3: Logistic Regression\n",
    "\n",
    "**Concept:** Despite its name, Logistic Regression is a **classification** algorithm. It works by calculating the probability that a given input belongs to a certain class. It's one of the most widely used and interpretable classification models.\n",
    "\n",
    "*   **Problem:** We will predict whether a passenger `Survived` based on their `Age`, `Pclass`, and `Sex`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "888b5d41",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "888b5d41"
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score\n",
    "\n",
    "# Prepare data for classification\n",
    "# We need to encode 'Sex' column\n",
    "df['Sex'] = df['Sex'].map({'male': 0, 'female': 1})\n",
    "\n",
    "# Define features and target\n",
    "features_cls = ['Age', 'Pclass', 'Sex']\n",
    "target_cls = 'Survived'\n",
    "\n",
    "X_cls = df[features_cls]\n",
    "y_cls = df[target_cls]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Split the data\n",
    "X_train_cls, X_test_cls, y_train_cls, y_test_cls = train_test_split(X_cls, y_cls, test_size=0.2, random_state=42)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "35c0906b",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "35c0906b"
   },
   "source": [
